### Transactional outbox — try it from VS Code (REST Client) or Rider/Visual Studio.
### Run the API first: `dotnet run --project src/TransactionalOutbox.Api` or `docker compose up --build`.
@api = http://localhost:8080/api

### 1. Start a subscription (row + outbox message, one transaction)
# @name start
POST {{api}}/subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "customerEmail": "dana@example.com", "plan": "Pro" }

### 2. A moment later Billing has issued the invoice
GET {{api}}/invoices?subscriptionId={{start.response.body.id}}

### 3. Outbox: every message with its attempts
GET {{api}}/outbox/stats

###
GET {{api}}/outbox?take=10

### 4. Simulate a broker outage (in-memory transport only;
###    with Docker Compose run `docker compose stop rabbitmq` instead)
PUT {{api}}/broker
Content-Type: application/json

{ "available": false }

### 5. Writes still succeed — the message waits in the outbox
POST {{api}}/subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "customerEmail": "eli@example.com", "plan": "Team" }

### 6. Pending, with attempts and the last error
GET {{api}}/outbox?status=Pending

### 7. End the outage — the backlog drains and the invoice appears
PUT {{api}}/broker
Content-Type: application/json

{ "available": true }

###
GET {{api}}/invoices

### 8. Cancel: a second message on the same stream, published after the first
POST {{api}}/subscriptions/{{start.response.body.id}}/cancel

### 9. Requeue a dead-lettered message (409 if it is not dead-lettered)
POST {{api}}/outbox/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/requeue
